.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH lpk 1  "lpk 0.0.1" 
.SH NAME
lpk \- LitePack archiver: create, extract and test .lpk archives
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBlpk\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-progress\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] <\fIsubcommands\fR>
.SH DESCRIPTION
LitePack archiver: create, extract and test .lpk archives
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Machine\-readable output: `l` prints one JSON object per entry per line, and any failing command prints exactly one JSON object line on stderr instead of `error: ...`
.TP
\fB\-\-quiet\fR
Print nothing but errors: no summary on stdout, no warning or note on stderr, no progress line, and no \-v report
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-progress\fR
No progress line. (The line is shown only when stderr is a terminal: never through a pipe, and not with \-\-json or \-\-quiet.)
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
lpk\-a(1)
Create an archive from the contents of a directory (not the directory itself)
.TP
lpk\-l(1)
List an archive, one line per entry: kind, size, mtime (UTC), path (tab\-separated)
.TP
lpk\-x(1)
Extract every entry of an archive into a directory; an existing file is refused unless \-\-overwrite says otherwise, and symlink entries are skipped unless \-\-symlinks is given
.TP
lpk\-t(1)
Test an archive: frame hashes, chunk hashes, Merkle root, records, recovery shards
.TP
lpk\-check(1)
Hash the data shards the recovery frames cover and report the damage (exit 2 if any)
.TP
lpk\-repair(1)
Write a copy of an archive with its damaged shards rebuilt from the recovery frames
.TP
lpk\-append(1)
Append the contents of a directory to an archive as its next generation: a snapshot (paths the directory no longer has are removed), deduplicated against the archive\*(Aqs chunks. A failed append leaves the archive as it was
.TP
lpk\-rollback(1)
Truncate an archive to the end of an earlier generation (in place; `info` lists them). Also recovers an archive whose last append was cut short. No key is needed: the credential flags, \-\-password\-prompt included, are accepted and ignored
.TP
lpk\-info(1)
Print an archive\*(Aqs header, envelope, counts and generations
.TP
lpk\-export(1)
Write every entry of an archive into a ZIP or 7z file (by the output\*(Aqs extension), streamed in entry order: no tree on disk, the blocks decoded as for `x`
.TP
lpk\-help(1)
Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)
.SH EXTRA
Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 usage error, 2 an operation was refused or failed. A failure is one line on stderr, `error: <message>`; with \-\-json it is one JSON object line instead: {"error":{"code":1|2,"kind":"<the error\*(Aqs variant name; Usage for a command\-line error; UnneededKeyfile for a \-\-keyfile the archive does not take>","message":"...","path":"<the path the error names, else the archive>","entries":[{"path","kind","message"}, the per\-entry errors of a partial extraction, when there are some]}}. A password is asked for on the terminal only with \-\-password\-prompt, or when stdin and stderr are terminals and the archive needs one that was not given; never through a pipe.
.SH VERSION
v0.0.1
